## Goal
Show how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) relate to each other in gene
expression, as a principal component analysis of the gene counts with samples coloured by
condition -- i.e. whether samples separate by condition along the leading components.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The counts file is gene x sample (200 genes, columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, A1..B3); the design
supplies `condition` per `sample_id`. Both resolved through `resolve_artifact.py`.

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib). Single script,
`scripts/sample_pca.py`, reading both inputs read-only.

1. Load the counts matrix; use `gene_id` as the index, drop `gene_name`. Load the design and
   check its `sample_id` set equals the count columns exactly (abort on any mismatch); order
   the columns by the design.
2. Transform: `log2(x + 1)` of the `length_scaled` gene counts. These are not integer raw
   counts, so a variance-stabilising transform (DESeq2 `vst`/`rlog`) is not used; log2(x+1) is
   the simple stand-in.
3. Filter: drop genes with zero variance across samples, then keep the 500 most variable genes
   (all of them if fewer remain -- the matrix has only 200, so in practice every non-constant
   gene is used).
4. PCA: `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=5, svd_solver="full")`
   on the samples x genes matrix, mean-centred per gene, **not** scaled to unit variance.
5. Write the sample coordinates (PC1-PC5 plus `condition`) and the percent variance explained
   per component.
6. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (two colours, colour-blind
   safe, with a legend), sample IDs labelled, axes labelled with percent variance explained.
   Saved as PNG and SVG.

No clustering, no statistical test, no interpretation of the separation is performed or
reported; the plan only produces the plot and the numbers behind it.

